WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.477 --> 00:00:13.202
Hi there, Little Fox readers.

2
00:00:14.082 --> 00:00:15.703
Sorry I'm in a bad mood today.

3
00:00:16.544 --> 00:00:20.566
But it's a really nice day, and my parents are making me go to a boring play at

4
00:00:20.606 --> 00:00:21.867
a stuffy old theater.

5
00:00:22.827 --> 00:00:26.769
My mom says it'll be fun, but I'd much rather be outside with my friends.

6
00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:30.932
Oh! That reminds me of our latest landmarks lesson!

7
00:00:31.904 --> 00:00:36.207
We just learned about a very famous outdoor theater—the Colosseum.

8
00:00:37.227 --> 00:00:43.311
Completed around 80 AD, this ancient amphitheater is a symbol of the once mighty Roman Empire.

9
00:00:44.352 --> 00:00:50.835
It's also the largest amphitheater ever erected in Rome—with enough seats to hold over fifty thousand spectators!

10
00:00:52.637 --> 00:00:58.100
Construction of the Colosseum began around 70 AD during the reign of the emperor Vespasian.

11
00:00:59.116 --> 00:01:05.520
Vespasian had come to power after Nero, a cruel emperor who executed lots of people and plunged the

12
00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:06.841
empire into chaos.

13
00:01:07.942 --> 00:01:14.166
Determined to restore order to Rome and pride to its citizens, Vespasian launched many public works projects.

14
00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:20.210
One of these projects was the construction of a magnificent arena in the center of the city, on

15
00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:22.672
land that Nero had once seized from the people.

16
00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:32.523
Following Vespasian's orders, teams of professional architects, engineers, and decorators designed the building, with about 100,000 slaves providing

17
00:01:32.563 --> 00:01:33.463
the manual labor.

18
00:01:34.523 --> 00:01:41.245
And because the ancient Romans had recently invented an inexpensive new building material—concrete—the new arena could be built

19
00:01:41.305 --> 00:01:43.046
cheaply, and pretty quickly too.

20
00:01:44.806 --> 00:01:50.348
Shaped like an oval (which is still the shape of most outdoor arenas today), the Colosseum had four

21
00:01:50.468 --> 00:01:51.288
tiers of seats.

22
00:01:52.416 --> 00:01:57.298
Workers created eighty entrance arches, so that big crowds could quickly flow in and out.

23
00:01:58.319 --> 00:02:04.381
The building was decorated with fountains and painted statues and even had soft cushions resting on its seats!

24
00:02:05.582 --> 00:02:09.603
Mr. Fay also showed us pictures of an incredible network of underground rooms.

25
00:02:10.644 --> 00:02:17.367
These were mostly used for storing supplies and props for the shows, including exotic animals like lions, rhinos,

26
00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:26.004
and—yikes!—crocodiles! The city celebrated the opening of the Colosseum with one hundred days of games and festivities.

27
00:02:27.164 --> 00:02:32.448
At first it was called the Flavian Amphitheater to honor Vespasian's family, the Flavian dynasty.

28
00:02:34.109 --> 00:02:40.113
But over time the building became known as the Colosseum because of the enormous, or colossal, statue of

29
00:02:40.193 --> 00:02:41.874
Nero that had once stood there.

30
00:02:43.035 --> 00:02:49.360
The Colosseum is pretty famous for its tall, decorative columns, which inspired many other architectural works around the

31
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:54.836
world. But if you ask me, the coolest thing about the building is its amazing engineering.

32
00:02:55.877 --> 00:03:01.742
Roman builders created lots of different machines to help shows run smoothly and keep the crowds comfortable; many

33
00:03:01.762 --> 00:03:04.685
of these devices are still used in theaters and arenas today.

34
00:03:05.686 --> 00:03:08.508
There were elevators and pulleys to lift equipment and props.

35
00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:15.634
Trapdoors opened under the floor for special effects, and on hot days, a special awning could be stretched

36
00:03:15.735 --> 00:03:17.076
over the building to block the sun.

37
00:03:18.888 --> 00:03:23.970
Engineers even designed a system for flooding the arena so that mock sea battles could be staged!

38
00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:28.091
It sounds like those ancient builders thought of everything, doesn't it?

39
00:03:29.271 --> 00:03:34.052
Admission to events at the Colosseum was free, but spectators still needed tickets for seating.

40
00:03:35.093 --> 00:03:40.054
And people had to sit in sections based on their social rank: Rome's wealthiest citizens got to sit

41
00:03:40.114 --> 00:03:45.296
in the first tier while poor Romans and women were stuck sitting far from the action, way up

42
00:03:45.336 --> 00:03:46.256
at the very top.

43
00:03:47.276 --> 00:03:50.598
By now you're probably wondering what kinds of shows they had at the Colosseum.

44
00:03:51.598 --> 00:03:55.620
Well I hate to tell you this, but most of them were pretty bloody.

45
00:03:57.321 --> 00:04:04.104
Mr. Fay said that as many as 700,000 people may have died there, along with even more animals.

46
00:04:05.264 --> 00:04:10.106
Sometimes so much blood was spilled, the sand covering the floor turned red.

47
00:04:11.147 --> 00:04:16.585
Most of the people who died at the Colosseum were gladiators—professional fighters who battled to the death to

48
00:04:16.665 --> 00:04:18.226
entertain Roman audiences.

49
00:04:19.446 --> 00:04:24.248
Some gladiators volunteered to fight while others, like slaves, were forced to fight.

50
00:04:25.289 --> 00:04:29.650
The sport was so popular in ancient Rome, there were even special gladiator schools!

51
00:04:30.851 --> 00:04:36.533
Other events at the Colosseum included animal hunts and exhibits as well as dramas based on mythology.

52
00:04:37.594 --> 00:04:42.456
People used to believe that Christians were martyred there, fed to lions because of their religious beliefs.

53
00:04:43.564 --> 00:04:46.327
But today most historians say this is just a legend.

54
00:04:47.307 --> 00:04:48.408
I sure hope they're right.

55
00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:54.814
Fortunately, the violent contests between gladiators were outlawed around 400 AD.

56
00:04:55.875 --> 00:05:00.339
In 523 AD the animal hunts and games were put to a stop too.

57
00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:04.442
With the end of the games, the Colosseum fell into disrepair.

58
00:05:05.523 --> 00:05:10.427
In the years that followed, it was turned into a cemetery, a fortress, and a site for religious

59
00:05:10.467 --> 00:05:17.670
events. Its grounds were constantly looted; people carried off stones, as well as gold and bronze objects.

60
00:05:18.811 --> 00:05:24.016
Today the Colosseum is one of the most popular tourist sites in the world, with millions of visitors

61
00:05:24.076 --> 00:05:24.556
each year.

62
00:05:25.597 --> 00:05:30.682
Although it's in ruins from all the looting, and damaged from air pollution and earthquakes, you can still

63
00:05:30.742 --> 00:05:32.164
see the outer shell of the building.

64
00:05:33.792 --> 00:05:38.776
You can also peek at the underground rooms and imagine what it was like when huge beasts and

65
00:05:38.856 --> 00:05:41.999
powerful gladiators prowled through those ancient corridors.

66
00:05:43.260 --> 00:05:47.483
In 2011 a restoration project was begun to help preserve the Colosseum.

67
00:05:48.564 --> 00:05:49.025
Like Mr.

68
00:05:49.065 --> 00:05:52.868
Fay said, it's important to remember the terrible violence that happened there.

69
00:05:53.929 --> 00:05:58.792
Still the Colosseum is a remarkable example of Roman architecture and human achievement.

70
00:05:59.873 --> 00:06:05.497
The famous writer Charles Dickens once described it like this: "It is the most impressive, the most stately,

71
00:06:05.837 --> 00:06:10.159
the most solemn, grand, majestic, mournful sight, conceivable."

72
00:06:11.179 --> 00:06:11.660
You know what?

73
00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:16.922
After telling you about the Colosseum, I feel a little better about going to the play today.

74
00:06:18.022 --> 00:06:22.564
The show might be boring, but at least it won't be violent and bloody like those gladiator battles!
